---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PROVIDERS, MANIFEST, freshness } from '../lib/data';

const title = 'Methodology — how RateCard collects and normalises prices';
const description =
  'Exactly how RateCard gathers cloud pricing, what it normalises, what it ' +
  'deliberately excludes, and where the numbers can be wrong.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="wrap" style="padding-top:38px">
    <div class="prose">
    <h1 style="font-size:27px;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0 0 6px">Methodology</h1>
    <p class="faint" style="margin:0 0 30px;font-size:13px">
      Data last collected {freshness(MANIFEST.generated_at)} ·
      {MANIFEST.compute_records} compute prices
    </p>

    <h2 style="font-size:19px;margin:0 0 8px">What these numbers are</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Public, on-demand, pay-as-you-go <strong style="color:var(--fg)">list prices</strong> for
      Linux workloads, in USD, before tax. Nothing here reflects an actual bill.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">Specifically excluded, because they are not comparable between providers:</p>
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>Reserved instances, savings plans, and committed-use discounts</li>
      <li>Spot, preemptible, and interruptible capacity</li>
      <li>Enterprise agreements and negotiated rates</li>
      <li>Free trial credits and promotional pricing</li>
      <li>Support plans, taxes, and currency surcharges</li>
      <li>Inter-AZ and inter-region transfer (only internet egress is priced)</li>
      <li>Boot disks, snapshots, load balancers, and IP address charges</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      GCP's sustained-use discounts are a notable omission: they apply automatically
      on real bills, so GCP's true cost for a month-long workload is lower than the
      list price shown here.
    </p>

    <h2 style="font-size:19px;margin:32px 0 8px">Normalisation rules</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      A month is <strong style="color:var(--fg)">730 hours</strong>. Where a provider
      quotes only an hourly rate, monthly is hourly × 730. Where a provider quotes a
      monthly price <em>lower</em> than hourly × 730, that price is a billing cap and
      is used as-is, with a note on the row.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Instances are grouped by matched vCPU count and RAM. That match is
      <strong style="color:var(--fg)">not</strong> a performance equivalence, and the
      table says so in two columns rather than burying it:
    </p>
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>
        <strong style="color:var(--fg)">shared vs dedicated</strong> — a shared vCPU is
        a slice of a physical core, often burst-credited (AWS <code>t</code>-family,
        Vultr Regular, Linode Shared). Sustained full load can throttle or cost more.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color:var(--fg)">thread vs core</strong> — an x86 vCPU is one
        hyperthread; two share a physical core. ARM cores (Graviton, Ampere) have no
        SMT, so one ARM vCPU is a whole physical core. An 8-vCPU Graviton instance has
        twice the physical cores of an 8-vCPU Intel instance.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      OCI prices flexible shapes per OCPU, where one OCPU is a full physical core.
      We record those as 2 vCPU each so the count means the same thing as everywhere
      else, and build the shapes to match the comparison grid rather than OCI's
      default memory ratio.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      GCP publishes no per-machine-type price at all — only separate per-vCPU-hour and
      per-GB-hour SKUs. Its instance prices here are reassembled from those components
      and marked <span class="chip chip-unverified">medium</span> confidence; the
      arithmetic is shown in each row's notes.
    </p>

    <h2 style="font-size:19px;margin:32px 0 8px">Effective cost</h2>
    <p class="muted">The headline number on the compute page is:</p>
    <pre class="card num" style="font-size:12.5px;overflow-x:auto;padding:13px;background:var(--bg-soft)">effective = instance_monthly
          + egress_cost(max(0, your_egress
                              − plan_bundled_allowance
                              − account_free_tier))</pre>
    <p class="muted">
      Bundled per-plan transfer is consumed first, then the account-wide free tier,
      then paid tiers in order. Providers pool allowances differently — DigitalOcean
      and Linode pool across the account, Vultr does not — which means a single-instance
      comparison flatters the pooling providers less than reality. Assume one instance.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources" style="font-size:19px;margin:32px 0 8px">Sources</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Prices come from official APIs wherever one exists. Nothing is scraped from a
      rendered page, and no provider's robots.txt is bypassed.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll" style="margin:14px 0">
      <table class="rc-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Method</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {Object.entries(PROVIDERS).map(([key, p]) => {
            const st = MANIFEST.providers[key];
            const method = {
              api: 'Public pricing API', 'api-key': 'Official API (key required)',
              'api-token': 'Official API (token required)', curated: 'Curated from official docs',
            }[p.collector] ?? p.collector;
            return (
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Provider"><a href={p.url} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">{p.short}</a></td>
                <td class="muted" data-label="Method" style="font-size:12.5px">{method}</td>
                <td data-label="Status">
                  {st?.state === 'ok'
                    ? <span class="chip chip-win">{st.records} live</span>
                    : <span class="chip chip-unverified">pending</span>}
                </td>
              </tr>
            );
          })}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="font-size:19px;margin:32px 0 8px">Freshness and verification</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Every record carries two separate timestamps, because an automated fetch and a
      human check are different claims. <code>collected_at</code> is when a collector
      last pulled the number. <code>source_verified_at</code> is when a person last
      compared it against the provider's page. Rows whose numbers come from curated
      documentation rather than an API are badged
      <span class="chip chip-unverified">unverified</span> until that human pass happens.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reuse" style="font-size:19px;margin:32px 0 8px">Reuse this data</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      The dataset and the collectors that build it are public. The price data is
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>
      and the code is MIT, so you can use either for anything, including
      commercially. Credit it like this:
    </p>
    <pre class="card num" style="font-size:12.5px;overflow-x:auto;padding:13px;background:var(--bg-soft)">Pricing data from RateCard (https://ratecard.cloud), CC BY 4.0.</pre>
    <p class="muted">
      If you change the numbers, say so. Stale or altered prices credited to us
      damage the only thing that makes this worth using. The raw JSON lives in
      <a href="https://github.com/ratecard-cloud/ratecard/tree/main/data/normalized">data/normalized</a>
      and the git history is a dated record of every price change since launch.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Publishing the pipeline is also the strongest claim we can make about the
      numbers: you do not have to take our word for how a price was derived, you
      can read the collector that fetched it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="independence" style="font-size:19px;margin:32px 0 8px">Independence</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      No provider pays for placement here, and no ranking is for sale. Where
      affiliate links exist they are marked as such, they never affect ordering,
      and every ranking on this site is computed from the price data alone.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      <strong style="color:var(--fg)">Hosting disclosure.</strong>
      Two companies in these tables are part of how this site runs, so both are
      named rather than glossed over:
    </p>
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>
        <strong style="color:var(--fg)">Cloudflare</strong> serves the site
        (Workers static assets) and runs its DNS. Cloudflare R2 tops the
        object-storage egress table, because it charges nothing for egress — a
        published fact anyone can check against
        <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">their pricing page</a>,
        not a favour. You should still know who serves the page telling you so.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color:var(--fg)">GitHub</strong>, a Microsoft subsidiary,
        hosts the repository and runs the price collectors on GitHub Actions.
        Microsoft Azure appears in these tables.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      Neither is a sponsorship, and no money moves in either direction: both sit
      on the ordinary free tiers any user can sign up for — no credits, no
      negotiated terms, no agreement of any kind. The honest position is that any
      host fast and cheap enough to run a site like this is a company this site
      indexes, so the answer is disclosure rather than pretending otherwise.
    </p>

    <h2 style="font-size:19px;margin:32px 0 8px">Where this is wrong</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Cloud pricing is a moving target and this index will sometimes be stale or
      mistaken. Known limitations, stated plainly:
    </p>
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>Prices converted from another currency are our arithmetic at a dated FX rate — the provider does not reprice when FX moves.</li>
      <li>Backblaze B2's free-egress allowance scales with stored bytes, so it cannot be modelled as a flat tier; its column overstates cost.</li>
      <li>Azure's default Microsoft Global Network routing is indexed; the cheaper opt-in internet routing is not.</li>
      <li>Bundled-transfer pooling across multiple instances is not modelled.</li>
      <li>Region coverage is limited to one US and one EU region.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      If a number here is wrong,
      <a href="https://github.com/ratecard-cloud/ratecard/issues">open an issue</a> —
      the dataset and the collectors are public, so corrections are a diff rather
      than a support ticket.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
